        {
            "kind": "facet",
            "key": "framework/data-cleaning-r",
            "version": 1,
            "layer": {
                "skills": {
                    "checking-data": {
                        "sync": {
                            "hash": "80754dc65931d80437aa1a614fc9b376693191e5d41368260d087e2c76f1f50a",
                            "server": "https://framework.pub",
                            "variant": "r",
                            "version": 1,
                            "skill_id": "framework:checking-data"
                        },
                        "bundle": [
                            {
                                "path": "SKILL.md",
                                "content": "",
                                "executable": false
                            }
                        ],
                        "content": "---\nname: checking-data\ndescription: Use when validating an analysis dataset, testing join assumptions, or investigating inconsistent counts, ranges, keys, and missingness.\nmetadata:\n  title: Checking data\n  summary: Assertions for keys, ranges, missingness, and analysis samples.\n---\n\n# Checking data\n\n## Validation\n\nDerive checks from the analysis contract: unit of observation, key, measurement units, valid categories, and time coverage. A successful script run does not establish these properties.\n\nCheck key uniqueness and required nonmissing values. Summarize missingness by variables and relevant groups. Test impossible values separately from plausible outliers. Do not delete an outlier solely because a statistical rule flags it.\n\nAfter joins, verify expected cardinality, unmatched keys, and row counts. After reshaping, compare group totals and the number of repeated measurements. For filtering, record the eligible population, exclusions, and final sample.\n\nUse assertions for invariants that must hold and summaries for conditions needing judgment. Report failed checks with counts and variable names. Avoid printing sensitive records. Keep validation code with the transformation or analysis that depends on it.\n\n## Language conventions\n\nUse `stopifnot()` or the project's assertion tools for invariants. `anyDuplicated()` detects repeated keys; check missing key values separately.\n"
                    },
                    "cleaning-data": {
                        "sync": {
                            "hash": "e61bfc90b137c45c25dbc7bc75eba9018c0df014e877d438855c0d4ff73b1656",
                            "server": "https://framework.pub",
                            "variant": "r",
                            "version": 1,
                            "skill_id": "framework:cleaning-data"
                        },
                        "bundle": [
                            {
                                "path": "SKILL.md",
                                "content": "",
                                "executable": false
                            }
                        ],
                        "content": "---\nname: cleaning-data\ndescription: Use when transforming raw observations into analysis data, including parsing, recoding, joins, reshaping, and missing-value decisions.\nmetadata:\n  title: Cleaning data\n  summary: Documented transformations from raw observations to analysis data.\n---\n\n# Cleaning data\n\n## Transformations\n\nKeep raw inputs unchanged. Write transformations in execution order and save the analysis data separately. State the unit of observation and expected key before joining or reshaping.\n\nPreserve source identifiers as strings when leading zeros matter. Distinguish absent, unknown, refused, and structurally inapplicable values when the source does. Record recoding rules beside the code. Do not infer units or missing-value codes from a few rows.\n\nBefore a join, establish its cardinality. Count unmatched keys on both sides and compare row counts before and after. Resolve duplicate keys explicitly; dropping duplicates is a substantive decision. For repeated observations, include the time or episode identifier in the key.\n\nKeep a small record of exclusions and row counts at each consequential step. Data validation and assertions belong in checking-data when available.\n\n## Language conventions\n\nUse explicit parsing types and `dplyr` join relationships where available. Keep identifier columns character. Compare `nrow()` and key counts across joins.\n"
                    }
                }
            }
        },
