                        "content": "---\nname: git-workflow\ndescription: Use when reviewing changes, committing analysis work, resolving merge conflicts, or coordinating branches in a research repository.\nmetadata:\n  title: Using Git in an analysis project\n  summary: Reviewable Git changes for analysis code and project files.\n---\n\n# Using Git in an analysis project\n\n## Git changes\n\nInspect the current branch, status, and diff before editing or committing. Preserve unrelated work. Stage explicit paths so generated output, raw data, credentials, and machine-specific files are not included accidentally.\n\nKeep commits focused on a coherent change. Review the staged diff and run checks appropriate to the affected work. Describe the changed behavior and relevant validation in the commit or review description.\n\nFor notebook conflicts, compare cell content and metadata using notebook-aware tools when available. Re-execute affected analyses after resolving conflicts. For generated files, establish which source produced them before choosing a side.\n\nDo not rewrite shared history or overwrite local work to simplify synchronization. Inspect divergence before merging or rebasing. Follow the user's authorization for commits, pushes, and publication. A request to analyze data does not authorize publishing its repository or outputs.\n"
                    },
                    "quarto-documents": {
                        "sync": {
                            "hash": "950ac20ed1541d246331352d2ac1228461d95b762409e923dd684164f7c52d6b",
                            "server": "https://framework.pub",
                            "variant": "r",
                            "version": 1,
                            "skill_id": "framework:quarto-documents"
                        },
                        "bundle": [
                            {
                                "path": "SKILL.md",
                                "content": "",
                                "executable": false
                            }
                        ],
                        "content": "---\nname: quarto-documents\ndescription: Use when creating or editing Quarto documents, including YAML options, executable cells, cross-references, citations, and rendering for R or Python.\nmetadata:\n  title: Writing Quarto documents\n  summary: Executable Quarto documents with stable references and reproducible renders.\n---\n\n# Writing Quarto documents\n\n## Quarto documents\n\nRead the document YAML and project `_quarto.yml` before changing output settings. Respect the existing engine and environment. Use executable cell syntax for the chosen language and unique labels for figures, tables, and cross-references.\n\nKeep computations in dependency order. Render from a fresh process to expose hidden state. Use project-relative input paths and avoid machine-specific working directories. Do not embed credentials in YAML or code cells.\n\nUse `fig-` and `tbl-` labels for references and meaningful captions that identify units and uncertainty. Keep citations in the project's bibliography. Preview the target format; HTML success does not establish that PDF or Word output renders correctly.\n\nUse caching or frozen execution only when its invalidation rules match the workflow. After changing data, parameters, or code dependencies, verify that affected results execute again. Render with `quarto render` from the project context and inspect warnings, missing references, and the final output.\n"
                    },
